¶
'''wretchedhead†''', ''n.'' [< ME. ''wreccheahede''; < ''wretched'' + ''-head''.] Misery; wretchedness. ''Rob. of Gloucester'', p. 102.

'''wretchedly''' (rech′ed-li), ''adv.'' [< ME. ''wreccheliche''; < ''wretched'' + ''-ly''<sup>2</sup>.] In a wretched or worthless manner; miserably; contemptibly; poorly.
{{smaller block|Thei lyven fulle ''wrecched liche''; and thei eten but ones in the day, and that but lytille, nouther in Courtes ne in other places. {{right|''Mandeville'', Travels, p. 251.}}
<poem>Nor yet by kindly death she perished;
But ''wretchedly'' before her fatal day.</poem>
{{right|''Surrey'', Æneid, iv. 930.}}
The defenses of Plymouth were ''wretchedly'' insufficient. {{right|''Lecky'', Eng. in 18th Cent., xiv.}}
He touches on the ''wretchedly'' careless performances of early comedy. {{right|''Amer. Jour. Philol.'', X. 268.}}}}

'''wretchedness''' (rech′ed-nes), ''n.'' [< ME. ''wrecchednesse''; < ''wretched'' + ''-ness''.] '''1.''' The state or condition of a suffering wretch; a wretched or distressful state of being; great misery or affliction.
{{smaller block|<poem>Is ''wretchedness'' deprived that benefit,
To end itself by death? ''Shak.'', Lear, iv. 6. 61.</poem>}}
'''2.''' Wretched character or quality; distressing, reprehensible, or despicable nature; aggravated or aggravating badness of any kind.
{{smaller block|<poem>Thy kynde is of so lowe a ''wrechednesse''
That what love is thou canst not seen ne gesse.</poem>
{{right|''Chaucer'', Parliament of Fowls, l. 601.}}
The gray ''wretchedness'' of the afternoon was a fit prelude to Barra. {{right|''Harper’s Mag.'', LXXVII. 782.}}}}

readings.c2s1:
¶
{{smaller block|<poem>We may fear he’l ''wrigle'' in
Twixt him and us, the prime man in her favour.</poem>
{{right|''Brome'', Queens Exchange, i.}}}}
{{smaller block|It is through these gaps that the people barely ''wriggle.''
{{right|''W. Besant'', Fifty Years Ago, p. 15.}}}}

'''II.''' ''trans.'' To cause to wriggle; twist and shake slightly and quickly; effect by wriggling.
{{smaller block|Their tayls with croompled knot twisting swashlye they ''wrigled.''
{{right|''Stanihurst'', Æneid, ii.}}}}
{{smaller block|When you wait behind a chair at meals, keep constantly ''wriggling'' the back of the chair, that the person behind whom you stand may know you are ready to attend him.
{{right|''Swift'', Advice to Servants (Footman).}}}}
{{smaller block|The Pi-Utes . . . ''wriggled'' their way out through the passages in the rocks.
{{right|''The Century'', XLI. 649.}}}}

'''wriggle''' (rig′l), ''n.'' [< ''wriggle'', ''v.''] '''1.''' The motion of one who or that which wriggles; a quick twisting motion or contortion like that of a worm or an eel.
{{smaller block|They [dapper men] have always a peculiar spring in their arms, a ''wriggle'' in their bodies, and a trip in their gait.
{{right|''Steele'', Tatler, No. 85.}}}}
{{smaller block|He was a person of sinuous, snake-like presence, and seemed capable of shedding his complete attire by means of one deft ''wriggle.''
{{right|''Harper’s Mag.'', LXXVI. 223.}}}}
'''2.''' Something showing the effect of wriggling or sinuous action; a sinuosity or contortion; a wrinkle. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|Minor folds and ''wriggles'' [in rocks] are frequent.
{{right|''Quart. Jour. Geol. Soc.'', XLIV. 11.}}}}

readings.c3s1:
''wringan'' (pret. ''wrang'', pp. ''wrungen''), press, strain, wring, = D. ''wringen'' = LG. ''wringen'', twist together, = OHG. ''ringan'', MHG. G. ''ringen'', wring, struggle, wrestle, wrest, = Goth. *''wriggan'', indicated by the deriv. ''wruggō'', snare; cf. Sw. ''vränga'', distort, wrest, pervert, Dan. ''vringle'', twist, tangle (''vringel-hornet'', having twisted horns); prob. connected with ''wrick'', ''wrig'', ''wry''<sup>1</sup>. Hence ult. ''wrangle'', ''wrong'', etc.] '''I.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To twist in the hands, as something flexible; twist or flex forcibly: as, to ''wring'' clothes after washing, to force out the water; to ''wring'' a friend’s hand in cordial greeting: often with ''out''.
{{smaller block|Mark how she ''wrings'' him by the fingers.
{{right|''Dekker and Webster'', Northward Ho, iii. 2.}}
Just help me ''wring'' these [clothes] ''out'', and then I’ll take ’em to the mangle. ''Mrs. Gaskell'', Mary Barton, viii.}}
'''2.''' To twist out of place, shape, or relation; bend or strain tortuously or twistingly: as, to ''wring'' a mast; to ''wring'' the neck of a chicken.
{{smaller block|<poem>His neck in twa I wat they hae ''wrung''.</poem>
{{right|''Jock o’ the Side'' (Child’s Ballads, VI. 84).}}
<poem>My spirit yearns to bring
The lost ones back—yearns with intense desire,
And struggles hard to ''wring''
Thy bolts apart, and pluck thy captives hence.</poem>
{{right|''Bryant'', The Past.}}}}
'''3.''' To turn or divert the course or purport of; distort; pervert. [Archaic.]
{{smaller block|Octavio was ever more ''wrong'' to the worse by many and sundry spites.
{{right|''Ascham'', To John Asteley. (''Encyc. Dict.'')}}}}

readings.c3s2:
{{smaller block|Or else they would straine us out a certaine figurative Prelat, by ''wringing'' the collective allegory of those seven Angels into seven single Rochets.
{{right|''Milton'', Church-Government, i. 5.}}}}

'''4.''' To affect painfully by or as if by some contorting or compressing action or effect; torture; rack; distress; pain.
{{smaller block|Wee know where the shoo ''wrings'' you.
{{right|''Milton'', On Def. of Humb. Remonst.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Oh, Portius! didst thou taste but half the griefs
That ''wring'' my soul, thou couldst not talk thus coldly.</poem>
{{right|''Addison'', Cato, i. 1.}}}}

'''5.''' To force out, as a fluid, by twisting or contorting pressure; extract or obtain by or as if by a squeezing flexure; hence, to squeeze out in any way; extort: as, to ''wring'' water from clothes; to ''wring'' a reluctant consent from a person: often with ''out.''
{{smaller block|<poem>He hath, my lord, ''wrung'' from me my slow leave
By laboursome petition.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Hamlet, i. 2. 58.}}}}
{{smaller block|The English government now chose to ''wring'' money ''out'' of Cheyte Sing.
{{right|''Macaulay'', Warren Hastings.}}}}

'''To wring off''', to force off or separate by wringing.
{{smaller block|The priest shall . . . ''wring off'' his head.
{{right|Lev. i. 15.}}}}

'''To wring out.''' (''a'') To force or squeeze out by twisting.
{{smaller block|He . . . thrust the fleece together, and ''wringed'' the dew ''out'' of the fleece.
{{right|Judges vi. 38.}}}}
(''b'') To free from a liquid by twisting or compression: as, to ''wring out'' clothes.
{{smaller block|And the Cabalists . . . say that Eves sinne was nothing but the ''wringing out'' of grapes to her husband.
{{right|''Purchas'', Pilgrimage, p. 19.}}}}

readings.c3s3:
¶
{{smaller|'''To wring the''' (or '''one’s''') '''hands''', to manifest pain or distress by clasping the hands tightly together, with or without a twisting motion.}}
{{smaller block|So efter that he longe hadde hyre compleyned,<br>''His hondes wronge'', and seyde that was to seye.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Troilus, iv. 1171.}}
She ''wrings her Hands'', and beats her Breast.
{{right|''Congreve'', Death of Queen Mary.}}
Under emotion we see swayings of the body and ''wringings of the hands.''
{{right|''H. Spencer'', Pop. Sci. Mo., XXXVIII. 11.}}}}

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' '''1.''' To writhe; twist about, as with anguish; squirm; suffer torture.
{{smaller block|Lat him care and wepe and ''wringe'' and waille.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Clerk’s Tale, l. 1156.}}
’Tis all men’s office to speak patience<br>To those that ''wring'' under the load of sorrow.
{{right|''Shak.'', Much Ado, v. 1. 28.}}
Such as are impatient of rest,<br>And ''wring'' beneath some private discontent.
{{right|''Chapman'', Byron’s Conspiracy, i. 1.}}}}
'''2.''' To pinch; pain.
{{smaller block|A faire shooe ''wrings'', though it be smoothe in the wearing. ''Lyly'', Euphues and his England, p. 474.}}
'''3†.''' To force one’s way by pressure.
{{smaller block|Thus out at holes gonne ''wringe''<br>Every tyding streght to Fame,
{{right|''Chaucer'', House of Fame, l. 2110.}}}}

'''wring''' (ring), ''n.'' [< ME. ''wringe'', ''wrynge'', < AS. *''wringe'', in ''wīn-wringe'', a wine-press, < ''wringan'', press, wring: see ''wring'', ''v.''] '''1.''' A wringer or presser; a wine-press or cider-press. [Obsolete or prov. Eng.]
{{smaller block|And erly sette on werkyng hem the ''wrynge''.
{{right|''Palladius'', Husbondrie (E. E. T. S.), p. 191.}}}}
